The rules it takes, each a pin: (1) a call that does not fit the budget inside a hole breaks its arguments one per line with a trailing comma, the closing `)` on its own line at the hole's indent - as a call outside markup already breaks; (2) a list literal that does not fit breaks one ELEMENT per line - its tuples whole where they fit - never inside a tuple while a sibling tuple stays inline (siblings alike: either every tuple inline or every tuple split, the same rule element children follow); (3) an element literal used as an EXPRESSION (a tuple slot, an argument) is printed inline when it fits the line, else split as a whole (`<span>` / children / `</span>` at the slot's indent), never with its open tag on one line and its text on the next because the LINE ran out; (4) a chain link after a call that was split goes on its own line, indented one level under the hole's indent (`print_split_chain`'s shape; the one-link width door applies); (5) a `css { … }` block as an operand of `+` keeps its block layout under the link's indent (today's, kept). The hole's braces hug the expression (`{select_component(` / `})}`) as today. Check the same shapes OUTSIDE markup (a plain `let` with the same call) - if the outside form already prints the wanted shape, the hole is the only seam; if not, the fix is in the shared breaking and the hole follows. Estate: run `vilan fmt` over kolt, the website's src, the examples and std on a scratch copy and list every line the change moves (expected: a handful of holes); the fmt corpus goldens that move are inspected one by one. Idempotence (`fmt(fmt(x)) == fmt(x)`) and the parse round trip pinned on the new shape. Sizing S–M (formatter.rs; fmt goldens).
